  "title": "Classical Black",
  "defaultTags": [
    "development"
  ],
  "intro": "# Classical Black\n\nThis is the setup you will meet most often: Black plays ...d5 and ...e6, builds a solid wall, and develops behind it. Nothing is threatened, nothing is sharp, and the game is decided by who understands the structure better.\n\n## What Black is doing\n\nThe pawns on d5 and e6 hold the centre and take away e4 and c4 for a while. The cost is the bishop on c8, which is stuck behind its own pawns. Black usually solves that with ...b6 and ...Bb7, and that takes time you can use.\n\n## Your setup\n\nBf4 first, then e3, Nf3, Bd3, c3, Nbd2 and castle. The bishop goes to d3 because h7 is a real target: Black has no pawn on g6 to cover it. When the setup is finished, Ne5 and f4 turn it into an attack.\n\n## The bishop trade\n\nBlack's main idea in this chapter is ...Bd6, offering to swap off your best piece. Answer it with Bg3 every time. If Black takes on g3, recapture with the h-pawn: that opens the h-file straight at the black king and gives you a free attacking road. Black has traded a good bishop for a good bishop and handed you a file.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nTaking on d6 yourself, which gives Black the c-file and a strong centre. Recapturing on g3 with the f-pawn, which wrecks your own kingside. Starting the attack with f4 or h4 before the king has castled. Playing dxc5 to release the tension, which hands Black a free game for nothing.",
  "stem": [
    "d4",
    "d5",
    "Bf4"
  ],
